{#
    A section of a document: a heading and everything under it, down to the
    next heading of its level.

    The theme draws this rather than takes what the renderer gives, and the
    class is the system's own. A page whose sections carry the name `section`
    is a page whose spacing has to stand against somebody else's markup. The
    one rule that gives a heading its air reaches a *sibling*, and the wrapper
    stands in the way. A box of its own is what lets the rule state the
    distance between two sections instead of guess it.

    A `<section>` and not a `<div>`: it is one. Deliberately unnamed — a named
    section is a landmark, and thirty nested landmarks is a page a screen
    reader cannot get out of.

    `:class:` travels through. To drop what a theme does not understand is
    the one thing it must not do.
#}
<section class="sds-section{% if node.classes %} {{ node.classesString }}{% endif %}" id="{{ node.title.id }}">
    {%- for childNode in node.children -%}
        {#- The part a section begins, named over its heading and after any
            anchor before it, so the pair stands flush — see `Parts`. -#}
        {%- if childNode is same as(node.title) and node.option('part') %}<sds-eyebrow label="{{ node.option('part') }}"></sds-eyebrow>{% endif -%}
        {{ renderNode(childNode) }}
    {%- endfor -%}
</section>
